 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566955 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566955 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566955 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566955 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566968 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c60aaa9a-9cd2-5978-a09d-624ed477578c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. The system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11dad216-cc7f-5ad1-80ce-080caf456bf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567017 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement: \n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e5e2349-4ced-536b-b394-0219c1de87f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567034 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 2$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]=4-2k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point.  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y=1 \\\\ y+x=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $7x+2y=1$ and $y+x=9$ intersect at exactly one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is not invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567049 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ kx+2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $x+y=0$ and $kx+2y=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. $k=2$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567056 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abbbffe0-a1a4-54a2-bcdd-88f376076c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567074 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b23db50f-6fe1-5c69-a46d-89da21fc9fd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567077 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-31908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb3fb3b9-076d-5aff-b850-4998889f64e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567100 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
